**A2 does not add the refused rule that the description takes priority, but its closing check is too broad: it can label an event’s own answer choices as “not judged.”**

For example, if the question asks whether an acquisition closes, the answer can already distinguish closing, withdrawal and neither by the due date. A check that demands *every other part* be listed as not judged can nevertheless list withdrawal there.

| Request instruction | Verdict against the cited standard |
|---|---|
| Ask one figure for a period or one event; judge what the outcome names as tracked. | **Consistent** with D397. It does not say the description wins when the outcome names several candidates. |
| Read the description and outcome together; list other parts **the question leaves out**. The schema repeats this. | **Consistent** with D397’s instruction to show the remaining wording as not judged. This does not resolve which part is main. |
| Keep a second side of the same bet as context and list it as not judged; that side alone does not require a decline. | **Consistent** with D436, while preserving D397’s decline when no part is main. D436’s own scope is clean Step 1, and it does not supply a general main-part selection rule. |
| Decline when no part is main, or the main part meets a listed decline case. | **Consistent** with D397 on no main part. The instruction does not itself choose a main part. |
| Decline an undated event without a named period, except the stated regulatory-decision exception. | **Consistent** with D403. |
| Decline another company’s figure or a matter without a listed theme; the remaining figure-period and year decline cases. | **No source in the three cited decisions.** These are retained from the frozen request; this audit does not establish their separate authority. |
| List a difference *within* an event outcome, such as approval with versus without conditions, as not judged. | **Consistent** with D397 when that distinction is genuinely left unresolved by the answer choices. It does not justify listing alternatives that the choices do resolve. |
| Closing check: list **every other part** of the description and outcome as not judged. | **Keeps wider** than the system text and schema’s “the question leaves out.” It lacks their limitation and can include the event’s own resolved alternatives. |

On the requested boundaries: two years presented equally still meet the explicit “none the main one” decline; another company’s figure and a matter with no listed theme still have explicit decline instructions. The added “second side” sentence could be misapplied to those cases, but it does not expressly override their decline rules. The sources remain silent on disputed cases where it is unclear which part is main, as the [earlier audit](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/model/rule-audit.md:1) found. These are readings of the request, not results from a new run.

NOT SOUND: The closing check can list already judged event alternatives as not judged.
