**Do not send yet.** The closing alignment is plausible, but it changes the request after the independent audit and needs its own blind audit of the complete revised request. The existing [audit](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra-a2/audit.md>) explicitly says it checked only three decisions and did not establish the authority for all retained decline rules.

Two checks also need strengthening before a send:

- **Test the known A2 harm directly.** The six gate controls check hard lines, but [the scorer](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-6/astra-a2/a2_probe.py>) skips controls before checking the listing classifications. A reader could label every listing harmless and pass. Add blind examples of an already-decided alternative and a settlement route, plus a sound listing; require both families to classify them correctly.
- **Check the actual request bytes.** The launcher compares canonical request identities, which sort keys. Compare the built-and-sent requests byte for byte, allowing only the documented text and schema edits. Test that an unintended change is refused.

Also make the product-review validation reject the same review file supplied twice, so “two readers” cannot be satisfied by one file. Recheck the cap and the revised controls after those changes. The ten exposed topics remain a diagnostic, and dropping the clean-ladder controls limits what it can show.

CONSENSUS: no send; independently audit the aligned request and fix the control, byte-comparison and reviewer validation checks first.
